    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d323">

                <pb facs="0378" n="364" xml:id="pg364"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Prime Minister</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Churchill</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to President Roosevelt </hi><anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d323-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-13">October 13,
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>No. 796. Your number 628. I have given instructions to the Food Minister that no
                    long-term contract is to be negotiated for the next couple of months or so and
                    that we are to proceed meanwhile on a month to month basis during which time we
                    can discuss the matters further.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d323-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>We are worried for fear that French, Belgian and presently the Dutch will come
                    into the market with plenty of gold. Nevertheless we shall make no arrangement
                    at present time but later on I must address you again on the subject.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d323-f1" n="5" type="source" xml:id="d323-f1-content">
                <p>Copy of telegram obtained from the Franklin D. Roosevelt Library at Hyde Park,
                    N.Y.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d323-f2" n="6" xml:id="d323-f2-content">
                <p>Text of the foregoing sentence amended as requested by Prime Minister Churchill
                    in telegram 797, October 14, 1944, not printed.</p>
            </note>
